 "substitutedBy": null,
 "summary": "Enacts the \"schools impacted by gross highways (SIGH) act\"; prohibits the commissioner of education from approving the plans for the erection of any new schoolhouse within five hundred feet of a controlled-access highway unless the commissioner of education determines that space limitations are so severe that there is no other site to erect such new schoolhouse.",
 "title": "Relates to enacting the SIGH act to prohibit the construction of new schools near a major roadway",
 "vetoMessages": {
  "items": [
   {
    "billId": {
     "basePrintNo": "A5735",
     "basePrintNoStr": "A5735-2021",
     "printNo": "A5735",
     "session": 2021,
     "version": ""
    },
    "billPage": 0,
    "chapter": 0,
    "lineEnd": 0,
    "lineStart": 0,
    "memoText": " \n                         VETO MESSAGE - No. 143\n \nTO THE ASSEMBLY:\n \nI am returning herewith, without my approval, the following bill:\n \nAssembly Bill Number 5735-C, entitled:\n \n    \"AN  ACT  to  amend  the  education law, in relation to enacting the\n      \"schools impacted by gross highways (SIGH) act\"\n \n    NOT APPROVED\n \n  This bill would enact the \"schools impacted by gross  highways  (SIGH)\nact,\"  which  would  prohibit  the  Commissioner  of the State Education\nDepartment or the Chancellor of the City School  District  of  New  York\nfrom approving plans for the erection of any new schoolhouse within five\nhundred feet of a controlled-access highway.\n \n  I  am fully in support of the laudable goal of this legislation, which\nis to reduce exposure to  vehicle  exhaust,  particularly  for  students\nliving  and  attending school in areas of environmental justice concern.\nIt bears noting that this  broader  work  is  already  being  undertaken\nthrough  efforts  of the Climate Leadership and Community Protection Act\n(CLCPA).\n \n  Unfortunately, this bill as drafted is overly  restrictive  and  would\nconsiderably limit educational options for students in urban areas with-\nout  taking  into account measures to mitigate environmental pollutants.\nIn the City of New York, where this bill would have the greatest impact,\nconstruction of new schools would be virtually stopped in  some  of  our\nneighborhoods  most  in need, where young New Yorkers already live. This\nwould result in students being forced to attend schools outside of their\nown community, added transportation time, and less available  space  for\ncommunity activities. Given the current space challenges facing New York\nCity  schools  and  the  need  for future space to allow for the smaller\nclass sizes required by legislation I signed earlier this year, I cannot\nsupport that result.\n \n  Instead, the State will work with  partners  to  pursue  the  goal  of\nimproving air quality without limiting educational opportunities for our\nstudents. To that end, Department of Environmental Conservation (DEC) is\nworking to implement the environmental justice related provisions of the\nCLCPA  centered  on  impacts  of  greenhouse  gases and co-pollutants on\ndisadvantaged communities. Section 7(3)  of  CLCPA  requires  all  State\nagencies to, among other things, prioritize reductions of greenhouse gas\nemissions and co-pollutants in disadvantaged communities and not to take\nactions that would disproportionately burden those communities.\n \n  In  addition  to  these ongoing efforts, the State has taken action to\ndrastically reduce  emissions  from  vehicles.  In  December  2021,  DEC\nadopted  the Advanced Clean Trucks regulation requiring manufacturers of\nvehicles greater than 8,500 pounds to sell an increasing number of zero-\nemission vehicles in New York State. I have also directed DEC  to  adopt\nstandards  requiring  all new passenger vehicles sold in the state to be\nzero-emissions by 2035, and in the FY23 State Budget we adopted new laws\nrequiring all school buses in the state to be zero-emissions by 2035  as\n \nwell as creating a zero-emission public transportation system and school\nbus roadmap.\n \n  I  look  forward  to working with my colleagues in the Legislature and\nour partners in the environmental community  to  continue  to  implement\nmeasures  that  will  reduce emissions, improve air quality, and protect\nNew Yorkers throughout the State.\n \n  The bill is disapproved.                       (signed) KATHY HOCHUL\n \n                               __________\n",
